# perf-optimizer: cut startup with an in-place CPU boost (no image/code change).
# Boot at {{boot.cpu}} vCPU (roughly halves startup), then resize CPU down to
# {{steady.cpu}} vCPU once the pod is Ready — 0 restarts.
#
# 1) In k8s/deployment.yaml, declare a CPU resizePolicy on the app container so
#    CPU changes need no restart, and set the boot CPU:
        resizePolicy:
          - resourceName: cpu
            restartPolicy: NotRequired
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "{{boot.cpu}}"
            memory: "{{current.requests.memory}}"
          limits:
            cpu: "{{boot.cpu}}"
            memory: "{{current.limits.memory}}"
#
# 2) kubectl -n {{namespace}} apply -f k8s/deployment.yaml   # rolls once to add the resizePolicy
#
# 3) After the new pod is Ready, resize CPU IN PLACE to steady (send the FULL
#    resources map — cpu AND memory — or the API rejects it):
#    POD=$(kubectl -n {{namespace}} get pod -l app={{deployment}} -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
#    kubectl -n {{namespace}} patch pod "$POD" --subresource resize --type merge -p \
#      '{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"{{container}}","resources":{"requests":{"cpu":"{{steady.cpu}}","memory":"{{current.requests.memory}}"},"limits":{"cpu":"{{steady.cpu}}","memory":"{{current.limits.memory}}"}}}]}}'
#
# NOTE: step 3 is a MANUAL, per-pod resize (fine for a demo / a single pod). In
# production, do NOT hand-resize every pod — use the Kube Startup CPU Boost
# controller (github.com/google/kube-startup-cpu-boost), which boosts CPU at boot
# and resizes back down automatically once the pod is Ready, across the whole
# Deployment. The resizePolicy above is the prerequisite either way.
